{"id":"tv-0383","task_type": "", "complexity": "","prompt":"hey so were seeing some wierd slowness on the storefront since around noon and i pulled the last bunch of client side timing beacons from the collector. i dont need a fix right now i just want to understand what the data says before i page anyone else. can you look at these and tell me which pages and which browsers look worst, and whether the slowness seems tied to one cdn pop or spread out. also give me rough avg and max for ttfb and load time per page if you can\n\n{\"page\": \"/\", \"browser\": \"chrome\", \"pop\": \"fra1\", \"ttfb_ms\": 180, \"load_ms\": 2100}\n{\"page\": \"/\", \"browser\": \"safari\", \"pop\": \"fra1\", \"ttfb_ms\": 190, \"load_ms\": 2300}\n{\"page\": \"/\", \"browser\": \"firefox\", \"pop\": \"iad2\", \"ttfb_ms\": 170, \"load_ms\": 2000}\n{\"page\": \"/cart\", \"browser\": \"chrome\", \"pop\": \"fra1\", \"ttfb_ms\": 210, \"load_ms\": 2600}\n{\"page\": \"/cart\", \"browser\": \"chrome\", \"pop\": \"iad2\", \"ttfb_ms\": 205, \"load_ms\": 2500}\n{\"page\": \"/cart\", \"browser\": \"safari\", \"pop\": \"iad2\", \"ttfb_ms\": 220, \"load_ms\": 2750}\n{\"page\": \"/checkout\", \"browser\": \"chrome\", \"pop\": \"fra1\", \"ttfb_ms\": 640, \"load_ms\": 4900}\n{\"page\": \"/checkout\", \"browser\": \"safari\", \"pop\": \"fra1\", \"ttfb_ms\": 710, \"load_ms\": 5300}\n{\"page\": \"/checkout\", \"browser\": \"firefox\", \"pop\": \"iad2\", \"ttfb_ms\": 650, \"load_ms\": 4800}\n{\"page\": \"/checkout\", \"browser\": \"chrome\", \"pop\": \"iad2\", \"ttfb_ms\": 630, \"load_ms\": 4700}\n{\"page\": \"/search\", \"browser\": \"chrome\", \"pop\": \"fra1\", \"ttfb_ms\": 260, \"load_ms\": 2900}\n{\"page\": \"/search\", \"browser\": \"firefox\", \"pop\": \"iad2\", \"ttfb_ms\": 250, \"load_ms\": 2800}\n{\"page\": \"/search\", \"browser\": \"safari\", \"pop\": \"fra1\", \"ttfb_ms\": 270, \"load_ms\": 3000}\n{\"page\": \"/\", \"browser\": \"chrome\", \"pop\": \"iad2\", \"ttfb_ms\": 175, \"load_ms\": 2050}\n{\"page\": \"/cart\", \"browser\": \"firefox\", \"pop\": \"fra1\", \"ttfb_ms\": 215, \"load_ms\": 2650}\n\nthanks"}
{"id":"tv-0205","task_type": "", "complexity": "","prompt":"hi, im new on the platform team and found this query in our ci metrics repo, its used for the weekly deploy health report. i dont really get the structure with all the ctes and id like a plain english explanation of what it computes and what each cte is for. nothing is broken as far as i know i just want to understand it before i touch anything\n\n```sql\nWITH recent_runs AS (\n  SELECT pipeline_id, run_id, status, started_at, finished_at\n  FROM ci_runs\n  WHERE started_at \u003e= CURRENT_DATE - INTERVAL \u00277 days\u0027\n),\ndurations AS (\n  SELECT pipeline_id,\n         run_id,\n         status,\n         EXTRACT(EPOCH FROM (finished_at - started_at)) AS secs\n  FROM recent_runs\n  WHERE finished_at IS NOT NULL\n),\nper_pipeline AS (\n  SELECT pipeline_id,\n         COUNT(*) AS total_runs,\n         SUM(CASE WHEN status = \u0027failed\u0027 THEN 1 ELSE 0 END) AS failed_runs,\n         AVG(secs) AS avg_secs\n  FROM durations\n  GROUP BY pipeline_id\n)\nSELECT p.name,\n       pp.total_runs,\n       ROUND(100.0 * pp.failed_runs / pp.total_runs, 1) AS fail_pct,\n       ROUND(pp.avg_secs / 60, 1) AS avg_minutes\nFROM per_pipeline pp\nJOIN pipelines p ON p.id = pp.pipeline_id\nORDER BY fail_pct DESC;\n```"}
{"id":"tv-0208","task_type": "", "complexity": "","prompt":"hey, got handed this prometheus rules file from the platform team and i\u0027m mostly a react person so the alerting stuff is new to me. can u walk me thru what each rule actually does, in plain words? esp what the `for:` and the `rate(...[5m])` bits mean for when someone gets paged. also lmk if anything looks off to you (thresholds, labels, whatever) but not looking for a rewrite, just a read-through so i can follow the PR. it\u0027s for our checkout-api dashboards\n\n```yaml\ngroups:\n  - name: checkout-api\n    rules:\n      - alert: CheckoutHighErrorRate\n        expr: |\n          sum(rate(http_requests_total{service=\"checkout-api\",status=~\"5..\"}[5m]))\n            /\n          sum(rate(http_requests_total{service=\"checkout-api\"}[5m])) \u003e 0.05\n        for: 10m\n        labels:\n          severity: page\n          team: payments\n        annotations:\n          summary: \"checkout-api 5xx ratio above 5%\"\n      - alert: CheckoutSlowP95\n        expr: |\n          histogram_quantile(0.95,\n            sum by (le) (rate(http_request_duration_seconds_bucket{service=\"checkout-api\"}[5m]))\n          ) \u003e 1.5\n        for: 15m\n        labels:\n          severity: ticket\n          team: payments\n        annotations:\n          summary: \"checkout-api p95 latency above 1.5s\"\n      - alert: CheckoutNoTraffic\n        expr: sum(rate(http_requests_total{service=\"checkout-api\"}[10m])) == 0\n        for: 5m\n        labels:\n          severity: page\n```"}
{"id":"tv-0428","task_type": "", "complexity": "","prompt":"can you tell from this per-service cpu/mem/rps/p99 table which of our python services have real headroom vs are close to saturating, and whether the p99 pattern looks like shared-dependency coupling or just per-service load\n\nservice | pods | cpu_pct | mem_pct | rps | p99_ms | db_pool_used_pct | p99_ms_1wk_ago\napi-gateway | 12 | 48 | 55 | 2400 | 210 | n/a | 180\nbilling-svc | 6 | 71 | 62 | 380 | 540 | 88 | 310\ninventory-svc | 8 | 39 | 70 | 900 | 260 | 74 | 240\nsearch-svc | 10 | 82 | 58 | 1500 | 390 | 41 | 330\nnotify-worker | 4 | 35 | 44 | 120 | 150 | 22 | 145\nreports-svc | 3 | 91 | 77 | 40 | 2100 | 93 | 900\nuser-profile | 6 | 44 | 51 | 1100 | 120 | 67 | 115"}
{"id":"tv-0307","task_type": "", "complexity": "","prompt":"can u turn these notes into an oncall runbook section for invoice-worker, steps in order w/ the actual cmds\n- queue `invoices` backs up -\u003e check redis mem first (`redis-cli info memory`)\n- if mem ok, check worker count `celery -A billing inspect active`, should be 6\n- stuck tasks older than 30m: revoke w/ `celery -A billing control revoke \u003cid\u003e`, then requeue via `python -m billing.requeue --since 1h`\n- dont restart all workers at once, rolling only, 2 at a time\n- if pdf renderer 503s, its the `render-svc` dependency, page platform not us\n- after fix, confirm backlog \u003c 100 in grafana invoices dash before closing"}
{"id":"tv-0312","task_type": "", "complexity": "","prompt":"can u tighten this release note draft for dashboard v2.14 changelog. audience = internal analysts + PMs, not eng. under ~150 words, group as New / Fixed / Heads up. dont lose the breaking bit abt saved filters, that needs to be obvious. keep tone neutral, no hype\n\ndraft:\n\nWe\u0027re super excited to announce a bunch of improvements to the dashboard in this release!!\n\nYou can now filter by multiple date ranges at the same time, which a lot of people asked for. The filter bar was also rebuilt using the new component lib so it looks different and is a bit faster on big datasets (we saw about 30% faster render on the orders dashboard in testing).\n\nWe fixed the bug where exporting to CSV would drop the last row if the table had pagination turned on. Also fixed the thing where the timezone picker reset itself after refresh.\n\nOne thing to be aware of: saved filters created before 2.14 that use the old \u0027relative date\u0027 option will not load anymore because that option was removed. Users will need to recreate them. We\u0027re sorry about this, it was needed for the multi-range feature. There is no migration for it right now, might add later.\n\nThe chart legend is also now clickable to toggle series, but only on the line charts for now."}
{"id":"tv-0024","task_type": "", "complexity": "","prompt":"hey need a terraform snippet for a new s3 bucket that will hold audit exports from our compliance tooling. requirements are block all public access on the bucket, turn on versioning, default encryption with a customer managed kms key (the key arn can just be a variable called kms_key_arn), and a bucket policy that denies any request not using tls. bucket name should also be a variable and i want tags for owner and data_classification passed in as a map. no modules please just plain resources in one file, aws provider v5 syntax"}
