{"id":"tv-0184","task_type": "", "complexity": "","prompt":"Hello, I am analyst in our monitoring team and I code only little bit. My colleague wrote this SQL query for the alert dashboard, but I am not sure I understand it correctly. Can you explain me step by step what it is doing?\n\n```sql\nSELECT service,\n       DATE_TRUNC(\u0027hour\u0027, fired_at) AS hour,\n       COUNT(*) AS alert_count,\n       COUNT(DISTINCT alert_name) AS distinct_alerts,\n       AVG(EXTRACT(EPOCH FROM (resolved_at - fired_at)) / 60) AS avg_minutes_open\nFROM alert_events\nWHERE fired_at \u003e= NOW() - INTERVAL \u00277 days\u0027\n  AND severity IN (\u0027critical\u0027, \u0027high\u0027)\n  AND resolved_at IS NOT NULL\nGROUP BY service, DATE_TRUNC(\u0027hour\u0027, fired_at)\nHAVING COUNT(*) \u003e 5\nORDER BY alert_count DESC\nLIMIT 20;\n```\n\nAlso, I want to know if the HAVING part is filtering the rows before or after the grouping, because this is confusing for me. And why we use the resolved_at IS NOT NULL, what is the effect on the numbers? Thank you very much."}
